 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "ROBIN HOOD. A bronze outlaw with a bow, striding out by the castle gates since 1952. His arrow has been pinched so many times that nobody has kept count. He is smiling anyway."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "NOTTINGHAM CASTLE. It sits on a sandstone rock honeycombed with caves. The old castle was pulled down after the Civil War; the grand house that replaced it was burned by rioters in 1831. It is much calmer now."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "THE CAVES. Beneath Nottingham are more than eight hundred hand-cut caves, used for cellars, tanneries, beer and wartime shelters. The pub next door claims it was founded in 1189. The caves keep their own counsel."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "THE LIONS. Two stone lions guard the Council House, and Nottingham folk say they will 'meet you by the lions' with a certainty that has seen off many a mobile phone. They're called Leo and Oscar. Neither has ever been late."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE OLD MARKET SQUARE. One of the biggest city squares in Britain, and home to the Goose Fair for centuries until it moved out in 1928. The Fair is over seven hundred years old and still sells apples the size of your head."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE LACE MARKET. In Victorian times Nottingham made most of the world's machine-made lace, and its merchants built tall red-brick warehouses to show it off. The lace has gone. Now they sell cake."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "THE TRENT. The big river of the Midlands, wide and brown and slow. On its banks stand two football grounds and a cricket ground, all rather proud of their trophies. The reeds are full of birds."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: John Boot opened a herbalist's shop in Goose Gate in 1849. His son Jesse turned it into Boots.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "nottingham_john_boot",
   "title": "John Boot's Shop"
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "nottingham_name",
   "title": "Name: Nottingham",
   "text": "Old English Snotingaham meant the homestead of Snot's people, Snot being a Saxon chieftain with an unlucky name. The Normans found the opening S awkward and quietly dropped it. The city has been grateful ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "nottingham_royal_standard",
   "title": "Charles I's Standard",
   "text": "On 22 August 1642 King Charles I raised his royal standard on a hill beside the castle, calling his supporters to arms, and the English Civil War began. Within days, it is said, a gale blew the flag down. Plenty of people took that as a hint."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "nottingham_luddites",
   "title": "The Luddites",
   "text": "In 1811, framework knitters around Nottingham began smashing the machines they blamed for cutting their wages, signing their letters 'General Ludd'. Ned Ludd was almost certainly made up, which made him very hard to arrest. Today a Luddite is anyone who distrusts new gadgets."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 44,
   "plaque": "nottingham_trent_bridge",
   "title": "Trent Bridge",
   "text": "Across the river stands Trent Bridge, one of England's great cricket grounds. It hosted its first Test match in 1899, which was also the last Test for W. G. Grace, the bearded giant of Victorian cricket. He was fifty, and decided his fielding days were done."
  },
  {
   "x": 69,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "FINGERPOST. East: Lincoln, its cathedral on the hill and the Imp inside it. Mind Steep Hill."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "landsky_ratcliffe",
   "title": "The last coal",
   "text": "On 30 September 2024 Ratcliffe-on-Soar power station, by the Trent just south of this city, burnt its last coal. It was the United Kingdom's final coal-fired power station, and its closing ended 142 years of making electricity from coal in Britain, begun at Holborn Viaduct in London in 1882. In 2012 coal still made about two fifths of Britain's electricity; by 2024 it made none, and the country was the first big industrial economy to give it up."
  }
 ],
